Carcinoid syndrome
Endocrine & Metabolic Disorders
Episodic cutaneous flushing, Secretory watery diarrhoea, Bronchospasm, Elevated urinary 5-HIAA

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: EMD-005
Reason for Visit: Severe episodic cutaneous flushing, acute exacerbation of watery secretory diarrhoea, and new-onset wheezing.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted acute onset of respiratory distress combined with continuous watery diarrhoea and tachycardia. Immediate out-of-hours assessment was required to exclude anaphylaxis, severe dehydration, or impending right-sided heart failure, preventing severe deterioration before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face feels like it is on fire, I cannot catch my breath, and the watery diarrhoea just will not stop."
History of Present Illness: Over the past 12 hours, the patient has experienced an escalation in episodic profound cutaneous flushing predominantly affecting the face, neck, and upper chest. This is accompanied by severe secretory watery diarrhoea with abdominal cramping, occurring up to 10 times today. Over the last 2 hours, they developed bronchospasm resulting in wheezing and dyspnoea. Symptoms acutely worsened following the consumption of alcohol earlier in the evening. They report a chronic history of vague right-sided heart failure symptoms including mild peripheral oedema and recent unintentional weight loss.
Functional Status: Independent, currently severely limited by respiratory distress and frequent bowel motions.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration and electrolyte imbalance due to secretory diarrhoea.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/60 mmHg fluctuating | HR: 112 bpm | Temp: 37.0 C | RR: 24 bpm | SpO2: 94 percent on room air | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Distressed, visibly flushed across the face and upper thorax, tachypneic.
Physical Exam: Respiratory exam reveals widespread expiratory wheeze. Cardiovascular exam reveals tachycardia, elevated jugular venous pressure, and a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border consistent with tricuspid regurgitation. Abdomen is hyperactive to auscultation with diffuse mild tenderness. Dermatological exam notes facial telangiectasias. Diagnostic tetrad features observed: Episodic cutaneous flushing, Secretory watery diarrhoea, Bronchospasm, and anticipated Elevated urinary 5-HIAA pending specialized laboratory testing.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty alcohol containers noted, directly linking the trigger to the acute symptomatic exacerbation.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient denies any regular medications. No recent antibiotic use or new food exposures.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Carcinoid syndrome - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic history of right-sided heart failure symptoms, the recurrent episodic nature of the flushing, and the absence of urticaria, angioedema, or known allergen exposure.

Acute asthma exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of profound secretory diarrhoea, intense facial flushing, and cardiac murmurs, which indicate a systemic rather than isolated pulmonary pathology.

Infectious gastroenteritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The prominent respiratory wheeze and intense vasomotor flushing symptoms, which do not align with a standard enteric infection.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The combination of acute bronchospasm, severe secretory diarrhoea, and profound vasomotor instability presents a complex and high-risk clinical scenario. Triage appropriately escalated this due to the respiratory distress and potential for rapid hemodynamic collapse. Objective evaluation confirmed active wheezing, tachycardia, and severe fluid loss. Delaying care until standard business hours risked irreversible respiratory or cardiovascular compromise. This immediate out-of-hours assessment was imperative to initiate bronchodilator therapy, assess fluid status, and differentiate from anaphylaxis, directly meeting the criteria for urgent MBS billing.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered salbutamol via spacer to stabilize acute bronchospasm. Commenced oral rehydration salts. Advised urgent presentation to the emergency department for further respiratory support and intravenous fluids given the extent of the secretory diarrhoea.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly avoiding alcohol consumption (action), you prevent the release of serotonin and other chemicals from the underlying tumour (cause), which will directly stop the severe flushing and breathing difficulties from being triggered (effect).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to oncology/endocrinology pending formal hospital workup.
DME/Safety (Optional): Spacer device provided for home bronchodilator use en route to the hospital.
Follow-up: Handover provided to the emergency department. Routine GP follow-up within 72 hours of discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Transport immediately to the emergency department. If breathing becomes entirely unmanageable, lips turn blue, or the patient becomes confused or drowsy during transit, pull over safely and call 000 for an emergency ambulance.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: This combination of symptoms heavily points to a neuroendocrine cause. Confirmation requires specialized urine testing and nuclear medicine scanning at the hospital.
Clinical Course: Left untreated, the continuous release of hormones can cause permanent damage to the valves in the right side of the heart and result in severe dehydration or respiratory failure.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Proceed directly to the emergency department now. Do not delay. Continue taking the bronchodilator via the spacer every 20 minutes if your chest remains tight during the journey.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The wheeze and diarrhoea are caused by hormones being released into your bloodstream, likely triggered tonight by the alcohol. The hospital will provide medications to block these hormones and stop the fluid loss.
Actionable Steps: Eliminate all alcohol and highly spiced foods immediately to prevent triggering further severe episodes. Drink small, frequent sips of the rehydration solution on the way to the hospital.

